Subject: faster JSON.parse and JSON.stringify on the paths apps use

What this does
  Speeds up JSON on the shapes an app actually serializes and deserializes:
  integer-heavy arrays, plain ASCII strings, and small nested objects.

What it changes
  - JSON.stringify: cycle detection moves from a JSValue array to a plain
    heap stack of borrowed object pointers, so push/pop no longer allocate
    or hit the GC. Integers are formatted straight into the output buffer
    instead of building a temporary JSString.
  - JS_ToQuotedString: a 256-entry escape lookup table plus a bulk copy of
    the runs that need no escaping, replacing the per-character loop.
  - JSON.parse: pure-ASCII object keys are interned directly from the source
    range, integer literals that fit in a double avoid strtod, and number-only
    arrays use a dedicated cursor path before transfer to dense storage. Arrays
    with a reviver or unsupported content use the normal parser.

Why we need it
  JSON is the serialization format React Native uses for the bridge and
  native bindings, so its hot shapes dominate real payloads. The engine's
  parser and serializer allocated per element and per character on paths
  these fast paths make more efficient.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No. This changes only runtime behaviour inside quickjs.c; the bytecode
  format is untouched.

